        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Central &amp; East Asia</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Scythians</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">The steppe and forest-steppe north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>; related peoples of a wider “Scythian world” lived east across Kazakhstan to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuva">Tuva</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">Scythian-type burials from c. 800 BCE in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuva">Tuva</a>; the Scythians of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> steppe from the 7th century BCE, at their height in the 4th; a Late Scythian kingdom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> until the 3rd century CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">Mounted archery; gold in the animal style; royal burial mounds such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chertomlyk">Chertomlyk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solokha">Solokha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tovsta-mohyla">Tovsta Mohyla</a>; the frozen tombs of Pazyryk; the failure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a>’s invasion</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>’s Histories, above all Book 4; Assyrian and Persian texts; the Hippocratic Airs, Waters, Places; excavated burial mounds and settlements from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuva">Tuva</a>; Greek-made gold showing Scythians; ancient DNA</span></div>
        </div>
