    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Scythians were Iranian-speaking horse nomads who from the 7th century BCE dominated the steppe north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, campaigned across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> and defied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>.</li><li>They left no writing of their own: we know them from Greek writers, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, who gave them most of Book 4 of his Histories, and from thousands of excavated burial mounds.</li><li>Their gold work of stags, panthers and griffins, and the treasures Greek goldsmiths made for their kings, belong to a wider Scythian world of related cultures that reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altai-mountains">Altai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> says the Scythians sent the invading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> a bird, a mouse, a frog and five arrows; one Persian read it as a warning that unless they could fly, burrow or swim away, Scythian arrows would kill them all.</p></aside>
    </section>

